From 79ee086d6d99f443b3bdce184f518f914c826ca0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: suiyan <suiyan@sunmmio.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 17:18:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] target/dadao: add SYS_read(63) cfx_smon responder (ML-025a)

Symmetric with the existing SYS_write(64)/SYS_writev(66) responders:
only fd==0 (stdin) is backed, any other fd leaves ret at 0 (same
"unsupported fd -> inert" convention those cases already use). Reads
byte-at-a-time via fgetc()/host stdin, writes each byte into guest
memory with cpu_physical_memory_write() (mirror image of SYS_write's
cpu_physical_memory_read()). Stops early on EOF or a genuine host
stream error, returning the actual byte count read so far (0 on
immediate EOF, matching POSIX read() semantics musl's stdio layer
expects) -- does not synthesize an error for EOF.

Needed because musl's __stdio_read (src/internal/dadao's musl) issues
a plain SYS_read for the unbuffered/getc-refill case, and previously
this backend had no SYS_read at all (fell to the default -ENOSYS
path), so no DADAO program could read real host stdin through any
buffered stdio path. Verified with a getchar()-based E2E probe
(dual-backend PASS, see tests/lit/E2E/musl_stdin_getchar.test in the
DADAO-0628 repo) -- reads back the correct byte values from real
piped stdin.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 target/dadao/cpu.c | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

diff --git a/target/dadao/cpu.c b/target/dadao/cpu.c
index d0cbf6d..d93c29c 100644
--- a/target/dadao/cpu.c
+++ b/target/dadao/cpu.c
@@ -142,6 +142,42 @@ void dadao_cpu_do_interrupt(CPUState *cs)
             uint64_t ret = 0;
 
             switch (sysno) {
+            case 63: { /* SYS_read: fd=arg0, buf=arg1, len=arg2 (ML-025a).
+                          Symmetric with case 64/SYS_write below: only
+                          fd==0 is backed (by the real host stdin FILE*),
+                          any other fd leaves ret at 0 (no bytes read) --
+                          same "unsupported fd -> inert" convention case 64/
+                          66 already use, rather than synthesizing an error.
+                          Reads byte-at-a-time via fgetc()/host stdin and
+                          writes each byte into guest memory with
+                          cpu_physical_memory_write() (the mirror-image of
+                          case 64's cpu_physical_memory_read() for the
+                          write buffer). Stops early on EOF or a read error,
+                          returning the actual number of bytes read so far
+                          (0 on immediate EOF) -- matches POSIX read()
+                          semantics musl's stdio layer expects; does not
+                          synthesize -ENOSYS/-EIO for EOF, only a genuine
+                          host ferror() is surfaced as an error to be
+                          consistent with "EOF is not an error". */
+                uint64_t fd = arg0;
+                uint64_t buf_addr = arg1;
+                uint64_t len = arg2;
+                uint64_t nread = 0;
+                bool io_error = false;
+                if (fd == 0) {
+                    for (uint64_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {
+                        int c = fgetc(stdin);
+                        if (c == EOF)
+                            break;
+                        uint8_t byte = (uint8_t)c;
+                        cpu_physical_memory_write(buf_addr + i, &byte, 1);
+                        nread++;
+                    }
+                    io_error = (nread == 0 && ferror(stdin));
+                }
+                ret = io_error ? (uint64_t)(-(int64_t)EIO) : nread;
+                break;
+            }
             case 64: { /* SYS_write: fd=arg0, buf=arg1, len=arg2 */
                 uint64_t fd = arg0;
                 uint64_t buf_addr = arg1;
-- 
2.43.0

